record legeos.observation.search-arrays-answer-broad-queries-at-two-million-in-under-20ms/1 : observation {
    title "In-memory search arrays answer broad one- to three-character queries over two million entries in p95 under 20 ms, where SQL takes 0.3-3.3 s, and match SQL exactly on real home data"
    state verified
    scope [
        path "crates/templar-file-index/src/arrays.rs",
        path "crates/templar-file-index/src/query.rs",
        path "crates/templar-file-index/src/schema.rs",
        path "tools/xtask/src/index_bench.rs"
    ]
    statement """
        Measured 2026-10-01 with `cargo run -q -p xtask --release -- index-bench workloads --allow-two-million` (record at .agent/scratch/integrated-indexing/workloads-2m-arrays.json) after the keys-first SQL search (stage 1) and the in-memory search arrays (stage 2) landed in templar-file-index. At 2,000,001 entries, first page of 100, p50/p95: `d` (1,999,968 matches) SQL 3,257/3,264 ms vs arrays 16.8/17.8 ms; `f1` (550,000) 1,169/1,171 vs 14.9/18.0 ms; `txt` (1,600,000) 1,728/1,737 vs 16.8/18.2 ms; `f12.txt` (50,000) 274/276 vs 10.7/11.9 ms; the path needle `d3/d4` stays on SQL at 688-710 ms. Building the arrays took 1.6 s at 2M rows and 60-83 ms at 90-99k. On a VACUUM INTO copy of the live home index (99,447 rows), arrays and SQL returned identical totals, ranks and order for 10 needles x 5 sorts x 2 directions (an earlier arrays build that assumed a directory's subtree follows it immediately in byte order produced 50 mismatches; `d-x` and `d.rs` sort between `d` and `d/`); `e` 234 ms SQL vs 2.4 ms arrays, `cargo` 28 vs 1.7 ms, `main.rs` 0.7 vs 1.4 ms. The SQL fallback for one- and two-character needles is slower than the old capped search (`e` 234 ms vs 126 ms) because it now counts and orders every match exactly.
        """
    observed_at git:e436ed2dc0a114429ec33a2cb621f6f58ccc64dd
    method instrumented
    watches [
        "crates/templar-file-index/src/arrays.rs",
        "crates/templar-file-index/src/query.rs"
    ]
    author "dk"
}

record legeos.observation.search-cost-is-linear-in-matches-and-truncation-is-not-best-first/1 : observation {
    title "Index search costs about 1.3 µs per matched row, mostly row materialisation and ranking; a truncated candidate set is not the best N, and folder-scoped Search1 drops in-scope matches"
    state verified
    scope [
        path "crates/templar-file-index/src/query.rs",
        path "crates/templar-file-index/src/schema.rs",
        path "services/templar-filed/src/search.rs"
    ]
    statement """
        Measured 2026-10-01 on a VACUUM INTO copy of the live index (94,953 live entries under /home/dk, release build, throwaway harness outside the tree). Index::search cost is linear in matched candidates at about 1.3 µs per row: `e` (like-scan, 94,928 matches) takes p50 126 ms with candidate_limit 200,000 and 63 ms at the default 50,000; `cargo` (FTS5 trigram, 14,691 matches) takes 19.5 ms. For `cargo` the FTS5 rowid lookup is 2.1 ms, entry_id after the filters 5.8 ms, entry_id plus sort keys (name_fold, path_bytes, size, mtime) 7.6 ms, and all ENTRY_COLUMNS decoded 10.9 ms; the remaining ~8.6 ms is rank_of re-folding every name and sorting whole EntryRows. The candidate LIMIT is applied before ranking, so a truncated result is the first N rows in FTS/rowid order, not the best N. Files.Search1 (the shell's folder-scoped search, limit 50) fetches the global top 200 and filters by path prefix afterwards: `lib` scoped to the LegeOS checkout returned 21 of its 24 matches. Storage: the vacuumed index is 148.7 MiB, of which entries_fts_data plus entries_fts_content is 88 MiB (about 940 bytes per entry), because the trigram index covers path_display as well as the name and stores its own copy of both columns.
        """
    observed_at git:e436ed2dc0a114429ec33a2cb621f6f58ccc64dd
    method instrumented
    watches [
        "crates/templar-file-index/src/query.rs",
        "crates/templar-file-index/src/schema.rs",
        "services/templar-filed/src/search.rs"
    ]
}

record legeos.observation.session-time-enters-sessiond-at-four-edges/1 : observation {
    title "Session time enters sessiond at four edges and leaves by one broadcast"
    state verified
    statement """
        Nothing in lege-sessiond knew when its own session began, was locked, was unlocked, or went to
        sleep. There was no logind signal match anywhere in the repository, and Lifecycle1.Lock only
        emitted LockRequested. So anything meant to age with use had wall-clock time and nothing else,
        which is wrong in exactly the case that matters: a laptop asleep for a week aged nothing, and a
        screen locked overnight aged nothing.

        Session time now enters the process at four points and leaves it by one. The four are:
        sessiond's own startup (Started, sent last in main so every in-process subscriber is already
        listening); Lifecycle1.Lock (Locked - the request, not a covered screen, because sessiond speaks
        no Wayland and cannot observe the latter); Lifecycle1.NotifyUnlocked, which only
        lege-shell.service may call because ext-session-lock-v1's unlock_and_destroy is the only thing
        that clears a lock and the shell is the only process that calls it (Unlocked); and one match
        rule on org.freedesktop.login1.Manager.PrepareForSleep, no polling (Sleeping(bool)).

        The one exit is a tokio broadcast of SessionEdge, capacity 64. The Lifecycle1 signals
        SessionStarted/Locked/Unlocked/Sleeping are a *view* of that channel, re-emitted by a single
        relay task that subscribes to it - so a client watching the bus and a host counting in-process
        cannot disagree about what happened, and no edge can reach the bus twice by two routes.

        Two ceilings are deliberate. Locked fires at request time, so a lock the compositor refuses
        closes the interval early - the shell tears that attempt down through the same release_lock
        path, which reports Unlocked, so the error is bounded to one frame rather than an open
        interval. And a session that cannot reach the system bus counts sleep as ordinary idle time;
        it says so once in the log and otherwise runs unchanged, which is the same bargain SystemLogind
        already makes for the power controls.
        """
    observed_at git:b1259f6fc8fc7973ce4090ab9f63af17d60b0206
    method command
}

record legeos.observation.shelved-tiles-have-no-route-back/1 : observation {
    title "A stashed tile has no route back onto the field, and a drawer already sits on the shelf unreachable"
    state superseded
    scope [ path "crates/lege-types/src/action.rs", path "shell/lege-shell/**" ]
    statement """
        The action manifest declares `field.stash` but no inverse. Delete shelves the focused tile, `lege_field` keeps it in `tiles` with no location, and the only way back is `field.undo` immediately afterwards — nothing enumerates the shelf, and no action, context-menu entry or chord restores from it. `FieldDocument::shelved()` exists and has no user-facing surface above it.

        This is not hypothetical on the developer host: the saved field carries the Recent drawer on the shelf, which is why Recent renders nowhere. `merge_drawers` cannot bring it back, because it skips any drawer whose tile already exists and correctly refuses to undo a deliberate stash — a tile shelved by accident and one shelved on purpose are indistinguishable once saved.

        The Window Shelf drawer covers minimised windows only. There is no equivalent for tiles.
        """
    observed_at git:53f7fd81e0e68bdff94978d180304fd0935509b6
    method observation
    watches [
        "crates/lege-types/src/action.rs",
        "shell/lege-shell/src/surface/field_screen.rs",
        "shell/lege-shell/src/surface/context_menu.rs"
    ]
    derived_from [ @legeos.evidence.home-field-reshape-across-resolutions-20260831 ]
}

record legeos.observation.shelved-tiles-have-no-route-back/2 : observation {
    title "A stashed tile has no route back onto the field, and a drawer already sits on the shelf unreachable"
    state superseded
    scope [ path "crates/lege-types/src/action.rs", path "shell/lege-shell/**" ]
    statement """
        Resolved by @legeos.work.home-shelf-and-representation-ladder: `field.restore` brings every shelved tile back, and `field.reset` discards a stored layout so the current default is adopted. Kept for the record because it is why both exist.

        As observed: the action manifest declared `field.stash` with no inverse. Delete shelved the focused tile, `lege_field` kept it in `tiles` with no location, and the only way back was `field.undo` immediately afterwards — nothing enumerated the shelf, and no action, context-menu entry or chord restored from it. `FieldDocument::shelved()` existed with no user-facing surface above it.

        This was not hypothetical on the developer host: the saved field carried the Recent drawer on the shelf, which is why Recent rendered nowhere. `merge_drawers` could not bring it back, because it skips any drawer whose tile already exists and correctly refuses to undo a deliberate stash — a tile shelved by accident and one shelved on purpose are indistinguishable once saved.
        """
    observed_at git:53f7fd81e0e68bdff94978d180304fd0935509b6
    method observation
    watches [
        "crates/lege-types/src/action.rs",
        "shell/lege-shell/src/surface/field_screen.rs",
        "shell/lege-shell/src/surface/context_menu.rs"
    ]
    depends_on [ @legeos.work.home-shelf-and-representation-ladder ]
    derived_from [ @legeos.evidence.home-field-reshape-across-resolutions-20260831 ]
    supersedes [ @legeos.observation.shelved-tiles-have-no-route-back/1 ]
}

record legeos.observation.terminal-workloads-inherit-the-terminal-identity/1 : observation {
    title "Everything a terminal starts is the terminal: identity is the launch unit's cgroup, there is no workload, and the governor sees only Templar launch units"
    state verified
    statement """
        At 50af378. Launcher1 starts an application as app-templaros-<AppId>@<uuid>.service in app.slice via StartTransientUnit (crates/templar-appd/src/systemd1.rs:75-150, crates/templar-types/src/unit.rs:11-44), and AppIdentity1 resolves PID -> /proc/PID/cgroup -> deepest registered instance -> AppId (crates/templar-appd/src/identity.rs:125-180). cgroup membership is inherited at fork, so kitty -> bash -> cargo -> rustc are all kitty's instance; a GUI app started from that terminal resolves to the terminal with VerifiedLaunchClaimMismatch (identity.rs:162-176). Nothing in resourced knows a workload, build or terminal job; its scan only discovers app-templaros-* units (services/templar-resourced/src/scan.rs:41-66) and its own test ignores an app-flatpak-*.scope (:82), and its actions are per instance (cgroup.kill, memory.oom.group). background.slice is configured but nothing is placed in it. The garden's bash PROMPT_COMMAND hook (session/profile.d/60-templaros-garden.sh) is the one per-command hook a Templar terminal already has. AppProvenance.sandboxed is reported and not acted on (crates/templar-types/src/variant.rs:215-273).
        """
    observed_at git:50af3784cb5c3f0ebe72a1e15f12a88f57e11290
    method observation
    watches [
        "crates/templar-appd/src/**",
        "crates/templar-types/src/**",
        "services/templar-resourced/src/**",
        "session/profile.d/**"
    ]
    author "dk"
}

record legeos.observation.the-hardware-packages-that-matter-most-arrive-only-through-recommends/1 : observation {
    title "Four packages a laptop needs for sound and Wi-Fi authentication reach the image only through a Recommends"
    state superseded
    scope [ path "distro/iso/build.sh", path "distro/seeds/selection.toml" ]
    statement """
        The pinned closure's own no-recommends experiment lists firmware-sof-signed, wpasupplicant, alsa-ucm-conf and thermald among the 415 packages that disappear if Recommends are ever turned off: NetworkManager only Recommends wpasupplicant and wireless-regdb, and thermald is a Recommends of linux-image-generic. By contrast intel-microcode, amd64-microcode, wireless-regdb and every linux-firmware-* subpackage are hard Depends of linux-image-generic and need no declaration. The four Recommends-only names are now declared in the hardware layer with reasons; the hard-depended ones deliberately are not. distro/iso/build.sh sets Apt::Install-Recommends true, so today's medium gets them either way — the seed lines are what survives a policy change.
        """
    observed_at git:cd2e8621d21088d8517c9bf7d4e342066aac4120
    method observation
    watches [
        "distro/seeds/selection.toml",
        "distro/seeds/closures/*.json",
        "distro/iso/build.sh"
    ]
    derived_from [ @legeos.decision.base-ubuntu-2604-kernel-70 ]
}

